3. V0 Capabilities — Preserve Them

Do NOT rewrite the working V0 functionality.

The existing V0 already provides:

3.1 Databricks Identity

The application can identify the current Databricks user/email using the current development authentication mechanism.

Preserve this.

⸻

3.2 Unity Catalog Authorization

The application currently uses Databricks metadata/INFORMATION_SCHEMA/privilege information to determine whether the executing identity has technical access.

Preserve this functionality.

The V1 architecture must continue to support:

* Catalog-level checks where relevant.
* Schema-level checks where relevant.
* Table-level checks.
* Column-level security as enforced by Databricks.
* Unity Catalog as the final technical authorization boundary.

Do NOT recreate Unity Catalog ACLs inside the application.

Do NOT grant Databricks privileges from FastAPI.

Do NOT bypass Unity Catalog.

⸻

4. V0 SQL Security — Preserve and Strengthen

SQL security was one of the two primary V0 goals.

Continue to enforce it in V1.

The SQL guardrail layer must be deterministic.

The LLM or Genie must never be trusted to enforce security.

Use a SQL parser/AST approach such as SQLGlot.

Do NOT use simple string matching as the primary SQL security mechanism.

Reject:

* INSERT
* UPDATE
* DELETE
* MERGE
* DROP
* ALTER
* CREATE
* TRUNCATE
* CALL
* GRANT
* REVOKE
* COPY where applicable
* Administrative commands
* Multiple statements
* SELECT *
* Unauthorized tables
* Unauthorized columns where application-level column restrictions apply
* Excessively complex queries
* Queries without required LIMIT
* Any query that violates configured query policies

Allow only approved read/query operations.

Return structured validation information:

{
“valid”: true,
“normalized_sql”: “…”,
“operation”: “SELECT”,
“tables”: [],
“columns”: [],
“warnings”: []
}

⸻

5. V1 — Business Access / Entitlement Layer

This is one of the most important V1 additions.

Create a Business Access Registry.

Its purpose is NOT to duplicate Databricks permissions.

It answers:

“Is this business user/business role entitled to use this Data Product through the Enterprise Data Gateway?”

Example:

DENTAL_CITIZEN_DEVELOPER
|
v
Dental
|
v
Dental Claims

Example configuration:

business_groups:

DENTAL_CITIZEN_DEVELOPER:
domains:
- dental
data_products:
- dental_claims

CLAIMS_CITIZEN_DEVELOPER:
domains:
- claims
data_products:
- claims

Do NOT store:

user -> physical Databricks table -> SELECT

That would duplicate Unity Catalog.

Instead store:

business group -> domain -> data product

⸻

6. Access Model

V1 must clearly separate:

Business Authorization

“Can this user use Dental Claims?”

from:

Technical Authorization

“Can the actual execution identity access the Databricks object?”

Business authorization happens in FastAPI.

Technical authorization remains in the source system.

For Databricks:

FastAPI business authorization
+
Unity Catalog technical authorization

For future SQL Server:

FastAPI business authorization
+
SQL Server technical authorization

For future Azure SQL:

FastAPI business authorization
+
Azure SQL technical authorization

This is why the core authorization service must NOT be Databricks-specific.

⸻

7. Identity Provider Abstraction

Create:

IdentityProvider

Interface.

Implement:

1. Local/MockIdentityProvider
2. Existing DatabricksIdentityProvider
3. MicrosoftGraphIdentityProvider

The V1 application should be runnable without Microsoft Graph access.

The current local/mock implementation should provide configurable user/group mappings.

Example:

alice@example.com:
groups:
- DENTAL_CITIZEN_DEVELOPER

bob@example.com:
groups:
- PET_CITIZEN_DEVELOPER

Later:

MicrosoftGraphIdentityProvider

will resolve:

User
->
Entra ID
->
Microsoft Graph
->
Group Membership
->
Business Access Registry

The rest of the authorization architecture must not change.

Microsoft Graph must be an adapter.

Do not hard-code Graph calls throughout the application.

⸻

8. Identity Caching

Do not query Microsoft Graph on every SQL request.

Create an identity/group context abstraction.

Allow group membership to be cached with configurable TTL.

V1 local implementation may simply return configured groups.

Future production implementation can use Graph + cache.

Never cache authorization decisions indefinitely.

